Package: RoyAI
Type: Package
Title: Generalized Roy Models of Human-AI Complementarity and Endogenous Selection
Version: 0.3.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Ibrahim", "Niankara", email = "Ibrahim.niankara@aau.ac.ae",
           role = c("aut", "cre")),
    person("BRASS Digital Lab", role = "fnd",
           comment = "LLC registered in Burkina Faso, RCCM BF-OUA-01-2026-B13-02466; https://brassbe1982-bdlweb.share.connect.posit.cloud/"))
Description: Estimation, simulation and diagnostics for generalized Roy (1951)
    <doi:10.1093/oxfordjournals.oep.a041827> selection models applied to the
    allocation of work across human-only, AI-augmented and AI-dominant
    production. Model A is a binary AI-adoption Roy model with endogenous
    selection (Heckman two-step and full maximum likelihood, with the
    classical Roy model as a parameter restriction). Model B is a multinomial
    Roy model with three production arrangements, estimated by
    multinomial-logit selection correction (Dubin-McFadden as modified by
    Bourguignon, Fournier and Gurgand (2007)
    <doi:10.1111/j.1467-6419.2007.00503.x>, or Lee (1983)
    <doi:10.2307/1912003>). Model C is the copula generalized Roy model.
    Model D is a copula Roy model for continuous AI-use intensity with a mass
    of non-users, estimated by maximum likelihood. Model E is a dynamic Roy
    model in which workers learn their complementarity with AI by Bayesian
    updating and acquire it by doing, solved by backward induction and
    estimated by nested fixed point or conditional choice probabilities
    (Hotz and Miller (1993) <doi:10.2307/2298122>). Dependence between
    selection and outcome disturbances is supplied by pluggable dependence
    families, so copula families and treatment-effect modules can be
    registered by extension code. The framework builds on Niankara et al.
    (2025) <doi:10.1109/GCET68529.2025.11450624>.
